# Terraform prod-apply safety (all agents)

Applies to any `terraform plan` or `terraform apply` run against `infra/aws/environments/prod`
(or any other live environment directory).

## Why this exists

issue-238 (22 Jul 2026): a plain, untargeted `terraform apply` would have destroyed the GitHub
Actions OIDC provider/role and stripped the live Redis AUTH token — both delivered the same night
by C318 — because `manage_github_oidc` and `redis_enable_auth_token` default `false` in the
tracked `.tf` files, but were applied as `true` via one-off `-var` command-line flags that were
never persisted. Nobody would have seen this coming from reading the code; it only showed up in a
full, untargeted `terraform plan`.

## Rules

1. **Before any `apply` — including a scoped/targeted one — run a full, untargeted `terraform
   plan` first** and read the `Plan:` summary line. Never trust a targeted plan's summary as
   proof the wider environment is safe; targeted plans only tell you about the resources you
   named.
2. **Any `will be destroyed` on a resource tagged for a live environment is a hard stop.** Do not
   apply until you understand exactly why, and confirm with the human before proceeding. This
   includes provider/role/replication-group resources gated by a `count = var.x ? 1 : 0` pattern —
   these are the most common source of silent destroys, because the boolean's *default* usually
   describes the pre-change world, and someone applied the *true* case once via `-var` without
   persisting it.
3. **When a change is applied via `-var`/`TF_VAR_*` instead of a committed `.tfvars` value, that
   is not "done" until the flag is persisted** (a new `*.auto.tfvars` file is a safe way to do
   this without touching an existing, possibly-sensitive `terraform.tfvars`) or the value is
   sourced from somewhere Terraform can read on every future run (e.g. a Secrets Manager data
   source), so a routine future apply — by anyone, or an automated drift-detection job — can't
   revert it.
4. **A secret-valued variable with no safe default is itself a landmine.** If a variable like an
   auth token has `default = null` and no other mechanism supplies it, *every* future plan/apply
   that doesn't explicitly pass it will try to null it out. Prefer sourcing the current value from
   Secrets Manager over relying on someone remembering `-var` forever (see
   `credential-rotation-safety.md` for the read-vs-write-scope pattern used for this).
5. **Splitting a monolithic `.tf` file into topic files is always safe** (Terraform doesn't care
   about file boundaries) as long as no resource/module block is renamed — verify with
   `terraform plan` before and after and confirm the `Plan:` line is identical. Converting inline
   resources into new *modules* is a different, much riskier operation (changes resource
   addresses, needs `terraform state mv` per resource) — treat that as a proper OpenSpec change
   with a written plan, not something to do live during an incident.

## Quick check before any prod apply

```bash
cd infra/aws/environments/<env>
terraform plan -input=false -compact-warnings -no-color | grep -E "^Plan:|will be destroyed"
```

If this shows anything other than the destroys you already expect and have explicitly signed off
on, stop.

